{"name":"Bubble Bobble P2","hex":"#0084ff","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bubble","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A spherically contained volume of air or other gas in a liquid, commonly a soapy liquid.","A small spherical cavity in a solid material."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce bubbles, to rise up in bubbles (such as in foods cooking or liquids boiling).","To churn or foment, as if wishing to rise to the surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bubble"},{"term":"bobble","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A furry ball attached on top of a hat.","Elasticated band used for securing hair (for instance in a ponytail), a hair tie"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To bob up and down.","To make a mistake in."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bobble"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bubble Bobble","blocks":[{"text":"Bubble Bobble is a platform game series originally developed and published by Taito. The first entry in the series, Bubble Bobble, was released in 1986 as an arcade cabinet. In most entries in the series, players control two dragons named Bub and Bob. This was followed by many sequels in both the main Bubble Bobble series, as well as the spin-off Rainbow Islands and Puzzle Bobble series. They were published for and ported to a wide variety of arcade and home video game consoles. The most recent release in the series is Bubble Bobble Sugar Dungeons in 2025."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/11/Bubble_Bobble_Logo.png/330px-Bubble_Bobble_Logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bubble_Bobble","wikidata":"Q10437156"}],"images":[],"sounds":{"bubble":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-bubble.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload